description:
Why: the pieces of a single-camera perception path exist but are wired together only in tests:
- ingest::foreground, a model-free background model;
- ingest::tracker, Kalman filtering with Hungarian assignment;
- ingest::eventgen, zone-gated hypotheses;
- recorded_event publication.
The CLI (fss-infer track) uses a separate IoU-only tracker, and fss-event report requires trained-model outputs. So an owner who has an MJPEG recording but no model cannot get a single event candidate out of FSS.

What: one command over a retained import, e.g. `fss-event watch --root DIR --site S --import-id sha256:... --zone id:x,y,w,h ...`. It runs decode -> foreground detection -> Kalman tracking -> zone eventgen, then publishes each candidate as an unclassified/indeterminate recorded event. That grants no alert authority, and a single sensor is never corroborated. It prints a report of the candidates with their evidence digests. It is deterministic and bounded, with typed refusals.

Tests, end to end on a generated MJPEG fixture:
- a moving object entering a zone publishes one candidate, and a rerun is idempotent;
- an object outside the zone publishes nothing;
- a static scene publishes nothing;
- corrupt input gets a typed refusal.

